WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.930
[Rollo yells]

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:05.470
Tyr Neilsen: Rollo's
using a Viking battle-ax.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.920
You can turn, you can pull,

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.560
and if it's used against
an opponent's legs,

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.600
it can be pulled up.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.370
There's lots and lots of tricks.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.540
Hi, my name is Tyr Neilsen.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.110
I'm president of the
Norwegian Glima Federation

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:19.068
and a senior instructor

00:00:19.068 --> 00:00:21.530
at the Academy of Viking
Martial Arts in Norway.

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:22.670
Today, we're going to be looking

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:25.358
at ax-fight scenes in movies and TV.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:35.540
There's different ways to
hold a weapon in single hand.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:36.373
When you have two weapons,

00:00:36.373 --> 00:00:38.470
there's a different thought process.

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:39.611
In this situation,

00:00:39.611 --> 00:00:42.320
I would use the ax with my lead hand,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.000
and maybe I would reverse
grip with another weapon.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.560
If I preferred to use the knife,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.980
then I would hold the
knife in my preferred hand

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.878
and try and cut as much
as I could with the ax.

00:00:53.878 --> 00:00:55.320
I'd follow up with the knife,

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.260
either in a forward
grip or a reverse grip,

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.262
dual wielding.

00:00:58.262 --> 00:01:01.730
Vikings, according to the
sagas, used two weapons.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.710
Sometimes they hid the
ax behind the shield.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.000
So, as you grip the shield here,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.380
you can hold the ax and
the shield handle here,

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.220
so it wouldn't be seen by the opponent.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.970
And if you drop your shield
or if it gets destroyed,

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.473
you quickly have another
weapon in your hand.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.240
This is very realistic choreography.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.348
He's using basic strikes.

00:01:28.348 --> 00:01:29.673
No sneaky techniques

00:01:29.673 --> 00:01:32.590
and no small, direct cuts.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.810
He's swinging with that intention.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.103
One of them could work.

00:01:36.103 --> 00:01:37.440
He only needs to connect

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.140
with the guy's arm or wrist or fingers

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.323
before you've created serious damage.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:52.870
The way they engage is
similar to glima techniques.

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:57.742
You try and trap the hand
that's being used against you.

00:01:57.742 --> 00:01:59.242
If it has a weapon, you try and trap

00:01:59.242 --> 00:02:01.270
the weapon or the arm,

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:02.611
and you use that to control

00:02:02.611 --> 00:02:04.019
the balance of the opponent.

00:02:04.019 --> 00:02:07.000
Lots of different styles
do similar things,

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.475
and my father-in-law

00:02:09.475 --> 00:02:12.391
began to teach me glima techniques,

00:02:12.391 --> 00:02:15.050
which are based on the
battlefield wrestling

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:16.580
from the Viking age.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.907
These are passed down quite secretly,

00:02:18.907 --> 00:02:21.730
because Norway has been
ruled by other nations

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:24.910
and occupied by Germany
in the second world war,

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:28.080
so their means of fighting hands-on combat

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.100
were held pretty close to the chest.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.400
It goes into grappling, and
the grappling was great.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.710
It was quite brutal,
and I really enjoyed it.

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:37.001
Seven out of 10.

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:49.590
It was a very tense and
quite realistic ax fight.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:52.930
You want to control your
opponent's weapon if possible,

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:55.256
and by grabbing your opponent's weapon,

00:02:55.256 --> 00:02:57.970
especially near the ax head,

00:02:57.970 --> 00:02:59.983
you can use it against the opponent.

00:02:59.983 --> 00:03:02.760
This is my favorite Viking ax.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.093
The toe edge would have been

00:03:05.093 --> 00:03:06.651
a couple of centimeters higher

00:03:06.651 --> 00:03:08.880
and then come to a lethal point,

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.600
and the heel would have
also come down to a point,

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:13.680
which would have been also lethal.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.880
And it would have been very, very sharp.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.154
This is a typical Viking ax.

00:03:18.154 --> 00:03:21.170
And then this is what
they call the bearded ax,

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:24.428
because this part of the ax,

00:03:24.428 --> 00:03:26.444
they call the beard.

00:03:26.444 --> 00:03:28.440
And it has an elongated beard,

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.200
and you can grip it here
and use it to shave or cut.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.260
And it can be deadly when
you move into this grip.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:38.900
When you close up properly,

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.920
you can use it as a knife
when in close combat.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.630
There are many decorated Viking axes.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:47.170
Vikings like to decorate
pretty much everything.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:48.900
On the haft, they would be protected

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:52.220
with leather, nails, or wrapped in metal,

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:54.440
and there's some examples from the sagas.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:58.070
This would protect the
wooden haft from any attack.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:01.395
Not always, because it's
wood. It would split.

00:04:01.395 --> 00:04:03.103
But it helps.

00:04:03.103 --> 00:04:05.010
It would be great if the producers

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:08.730
got in contact with people
who fight in the style

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:10.910
that the people in the show
were going to be fighting in,

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.780
because there's a lot of
information and techniques there.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:17.530
You can use that as a platform
to make better choreography.

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:20.860
This was very exciting, quite realistic,

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:24.703
and a great starter for the whole show.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.960
Rollo's using a Viking battle-ax,

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:34.100
which is an incredibly dangerous weapon.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.340
The ax head goes through the shield.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:38.110
It can be a dangerous move.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:41.380
If the ax head gets stuck in the shield,

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:42.860
it can be used against you.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:45.851
I have a Viking battle-ax,

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:47.451
also called a Dane ax.

00:04:47.451 --> 00:04:49.950
And if you imagine my arm is a shield,

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:53.060
you can hook over the top of
the shield and pull it down,

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:55.420
and you can do it really quickly.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.310
This is just a small ax head.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.490
You can turn, you can pull,

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.150
and if it's used against
an opponent's legs,

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:04.280
it can be pulled up.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:06.780
You can push forward
to open up the shield.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.484
There's lots and lots of tricks with this.

00:05:09.484 --> 00:05:12.800
And you can use the ax as a stick.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.400
You can use either this end or this end,

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:20.151
or you can use it as a stick
weapon here and then turn.

00:05:21.153 --> 00:05:25.840
It's made to be maneuvered around.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.120
If you start here, you
can deceive a little

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.028
and then send it out quite a long way.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.363
He uses it with technique, strength.

00:05:35.363 --> 00:05:37.320
Seven out of 10.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:41.230
I believe Rollo, and, the
character from the show,

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:43.910
I really wouldn't want
to meet him in a fight.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.730
Viking boys, growing up,
they get bored, as kids do,

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:54.630
and they would start wrestling,

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:56.104
fighting with weapons.

00:05:56.104 --> 00:05:58.430
Every one of them was quite good

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:00.560
at throwing axes, throwing knives.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:01.920
When we're doing seminars,

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.310
we're not instructing
and we're hanging around,

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.390
people throw their weapons,

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:07.890
they throw their axes,
they throw their knives.

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:09.680
It's a very natural thing to do.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.020
I could throw the ax with
pretty good accuracy.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:16.160
I don't know if I would
cut the ropes first time,

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.426
but I would imagine that

00:06:18.426 --> 00:06:21.380
that character could and would.

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:23.013
And as he did,

00:06:23.013 --> 00:06:25.488
10 out of 10, because he cut the ropes.

00:06:31.996 --> 00:06:35.720
I don't know anybody who
would swing a two-bladed

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:38.190
or two-bit ax around their body like this.

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:40.360
It would be too easy to
cut off your own limb.

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:42.135
It could get caught in material.

00:06:42.135 --> 00:06:46.310
It could rebound. It could be deflected.

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:48.910
There's lots of things that
can happen unexpectedly.

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:54.770
I don't know the weight of the weapon,

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:57.130
I don't know what type of
material it's made out of,

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:59.190
but if you're living 6,000 years,

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:00.520
you've had a lot of practice.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.140
You're pretty much invulnerable.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:03.843
You could do this. If you're showing off,

00:07:03.843 --> 00:07:07.901
if you're building up the
energy, the determination,

00:07:07.901 --> 00:07:10.220
might happen, but I wouldn't recommend it.

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:13.150
And I think that this
is based on the labrys,

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:16.424
which is a ancient weapon

00:07:16.424 --> 00:07:18.857
from Crete, Greece.

00:07:18.857 --> 00:07:20.790
It was more of a ceremonial weapon

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:22.690
than anything that they
would use in battle.

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:24.723
There's a lot of discourse about

00:07:24.723 --> 00:07:28.060
the pros and cons of this
and what it was used for.

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:30.115
In a sacred ceremony,
it might've been used

00:07:30.115 --> 00:07:33.160
to sacrifice an animal, for example.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:35.240
The choreography is interesting.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.200
Because she didn't cut off any limbs,

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:38.700
she gets 10 out of 10.

00:07:43.819 --> 00:07:45.141
[laughing]

00:07:45.141 --> 00:07:46.016
Hm.

00:07:46.016 --> 00:07:47.500
Well, I don't think anybody ever

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:48.920
wants to be put in the position

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:51.420
where they have to
criticize a John Wick movie.

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:53.840
It looks like they're
using a woodsman's ax

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.763
or something about this size.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.490
It weighs between 1 1/2, 2 pounds,

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:03.660
it's really easy to swing around,

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:07.321
and it's very lethal,
especially in short distances

00:08:07.321 --> 00:08:09.550
and in a compact room, the way this is.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:11.520
I doubt that they're sharp.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:12.920
If we believe the scene,

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:15.680
this is an incredibly scary situation.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.103
He's doing really good techniques,

00:08:17.103 --> 00:08:20.236
blocking, powering, moving
out of the way. I love it.

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:26.860
You have to trap and lock it away.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:29.840
You have to make sure that
you're not at the distance

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:31.730
where you can easily be cut by it.

00:08:31.730 --> 00:08:33.420
Maybe get inside the weapon.

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:35.760
In glima, there's an
awful lot of techniques

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:40.410
we use to intercept, to trap, to hold,

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:42.250
to maneuver the opponent's body weight

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:44.960
in such a way that they
don't have the best stability

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:46.630
so that they can use the weapon.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:48.751
I really can't find a fault

00:08:48.751 --> 00:08:51.250
or say if you did this it would be better,

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:52.674
so 10 out of 10.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:59.590
He's using a long-hafted ax.

00:08:59.590 --> 00:09:00.990
He's going to have to do something

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:04.040
to change the disadvantage of his height

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:05.070
into an advantage,

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:07.380
and this is a really good way of doing it.

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:10.690
He can reach up high because
he's using that weapon.

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:13.520
I would suggest that he
cuts more ankles and legs,

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:14.600
because that's easier.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.080
And if your opponent can't walk,

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:19.063
then they can't run
after you or attack you.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:26.745
The way he's using the ax is
an extension of his character,

00:09:26.745 --> 00:09:29.360
and he's trying to kill with every blow.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:31.990
He wants to show that he's powerful

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:33.350
with every single stroke.

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:37.420
The problem with swinging
heavily every time

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:40.750
is that you drain your
energy, which is limited.

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:43.710
You open yourself up if you bring it back

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:45.310
in order to swing it forward.

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:49.650
So, this is a regular modern tomahawk ax.

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:52.080
And instead of it being
brought all the way back

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.863
in order to go forward,

00:09:55.747 --> 00:09:57.388
that would be a move.

00:09:57.388 --> 00:09:58.670
And this is quite sharp.

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:00.370
The tips are quite sharp.

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:01.330
It's heavy enough.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:03.685
It's got this at the end,

00:10:03.685 --> 00:10:07.053
and that would be doing quite
a lot of damage from here.

00:10:08.706 --> 00:10:11.200
And you don't really need
to do much more than that,

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:13.320
if you understand what you're attacking.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:14.740
And when you chuck it....

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:19.873
And the way he uses it, seven out of 10.

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:28.340
Chris Evans sells this so well.

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.530
He's a good actor who can sell action.

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:32.800
He changes hands,

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.751
he goes from single grip to double grip,

00:10:35.751 --> 00:10:40.140
he has good turns and good angles,

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:41.990
he changes levels.

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.870
A good fighter will try and create

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:45.900
as much space around them as possible.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.810
Try and use opponents' shields,

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:52.543
move around them in a way that
they shield or block for you.

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:00.520
The most difficult thing of
all is to keep your balance.

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:01.970
And then he does what he should do,

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:03.543
which is attack the legs

00:11:03.543 --> 00:11:06.860
and try and get out of
there as soon as possible.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:09.700
We don't play any defensive
game on the ground.

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:13.316
We don't fall; we're taught to land.

00:11:13.316 --> 00:11:15.610
And so no matter what
happens, as you're going down,

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:18.633
you're going down with the intention

00:11:18.633 --> 00:11:20.622
to land in a way that will enable you

00:11:20.622 --> 00:11:23.730
to move out of danger
as quickly as possible.

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.173
Nine out of 10.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:32.710
If somebody has that much confidence

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:36.330
and swings it around in such a
way that it doesn't look like

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:38.352
they're going to injure themselves at all

00:11:38.352 --> 00:11:41.400
and you do not know where the
attack is going to come from,

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:44.490
what happens is it's hard
to see the angle of attack.

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:47.060
Whichever way they begin the movement,

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:49.263
there's many, many tells, from the neck,

00:11:49.263 --> 00:11:52.430
the shoulder, the bicep,
the tricep, the elbow.

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:53.821
There's lots and lots of tells.

00:11:53.821 --> 00:11:55.287
The way they move their feet,

00:11:55.287 --> 00:11:56.880
the way they move their head.

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:00.280
But when that weapon is already in motion,

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:04.120
you can't just attack or
defend against that angle,

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:06.140
because it can suddenly change.

00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:07.693
It's intimidating.

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:16.840
Where you place your feet,
when I look at technique,

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:19.780
you can see what's happening
just from the footwork.

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:23.750
Footwork is a major
part of any martial art.

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:27.140
It's often neglected
because it's not as exciting

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:29.550
as twirling your weapon
around in your hand,

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:32.750
but being able to move at angles,

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:34.770
move forward, move backwards,

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:36.780
move as you change your height,

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:40.200
it's so, so important. It's core.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:41.438
Balance is everything.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:47.730
They use their weapon to
hook the opponent's weapon

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:49.538
and control the opponent's weapon

00:12:49.538 --> 00:12:51.138
and the opponent's balance.

00:12:51.138 --> 00:12:52.670
If I can just show it here,

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:55.920
the opponent's weapon,
you go over the top,

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:57.830
you can either pull and go forward

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:00.580
or just go over the top and go forward.

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:03.110
You don't need these big movements.

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:05.126
So, over the weapon,

00:13:05.126 --> 00:13:07.292
force it in a direction away from you,

00:13:07.292 --> 00:13:08.400
and that's all you need.

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:10.190
I felt it was really well done

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:13.640
and it was scary and good technique.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:14.990
Eight out of 10.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:26.470
He leaves himself open
because he is full of bravado,

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:27.700
but it works really well

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:30.660
because even though he opens himself up,

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:32.110
he knows exactly what to do

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:34.103
to close it up again and attacking you.

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:40.953
In this scene, Ajax cuts through

00:13:40.953 --> 00:13:43.060
a good chunk of metal with his ax.

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:46.090
It could happen, maybe. I haven't tried.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:47.590
These are dangerous weapons,

00:13:47.590 --> 00:13:49.970
and they can cut through quite a lot.

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:52.140
I think this is based on a fireman's ax.

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:54.873
I've never seen one which
is completely metal.

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:00.855
There's different rhythms in a fight,

00:14:00.855 --> 00:14:02.870
and you can change rhythm in a fight.

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:06.300
Instead of getting dragged
into a readable rhythm,

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:07.520
you can break the rhythm.

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:10.197
And then you change the
dynamic of the fight

00:14:10.197 --> 00:14:14.930
by changing the speed of
one weapon against another

00:14:14.930 --> 00:14:18.716
and getting them in
unison or working apart.

00:14:18.716 --> 00:14:22.260
A delayed swing changes everything.

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:23.987
I'd give this nine out of 10.

00:14:23.987 --> 00:14:25.371
I really enjoyed it.

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:34.060
I've watched this with my son many times.

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:36.900
We really enjoy this scene.

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:39.860
The way he moves is impressive.

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:42.260
If your opponent can move quickly,

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:44.093
if they know how to use a weapon,

00:14:44.093 --> 00:14:45.768
that's a game changer.

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:53.600
The fact that you can
use an anonymous object

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:58.560
as a weapon or as a counter
to these weapons is smart.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:02.240
Maybe going in and
smothering your opponent

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:05.610
and trying to strip their
weapon away would work.

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:08.120
But you also have a second attacker

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:10.690
coming in with another ax.

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:14.140
There's no sure way that
you come out of this alive,

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:16.700
but as a scene, it's inventive.

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:19.520
We do train against multiple attackers

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:21.630
and multiple attackers with weapons.

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:23.940
One of the basic principles
that you have to understand

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:27.040
is that you want to be able
to control your opponents

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:30.290
and use that opponent to
control a second opponent.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:32.093
Use them as a shield,

00:15:32.093 --> 00:15:35.810
use their weapon as a
counterweapon or as a shield.

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:38.526
I'd like to give this 10 out of 10.

00:15:38.526 --> 00:15:40.623
I just thoroughly enjoyed it.

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:51.190
It is always enjoyable
to see somebody move

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:53.460
the way she moves in this scene.

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:55.170
I think this was one long take.

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:57.380
She's elegant. She's determined.

00:15:57.380 --> 00:15:59.180
And when you're using weapons,

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:00.850
they should be an extension of your body,

00:16:00.850 --> 00:16:05.083
and this scene really
shows that part of a fight.

00:16:11.225 --> 00:16:14.300
I wouldn't recommend
anyone fighting this way,

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:18.150
but if you're a person
with these capabilities

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:19.500
and this is your style,

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:21.150
then you can make it work for you.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:22.310
I would not do this.

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:25.160
The style that I train and teach

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:27.780
is about making the best decisions

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:29.530
in the shortest possible time.

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:31.890
I think she's using a fantasy weapon.

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:33.780
I don't know what it's based on,

00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:37.620
but the Vikings had a similar type of ax

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:39.550
where there's a long beard,

00:16:39.550 --> 00:16:41.550
which is used for carving or cutting.

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:42.920
If it has a long tang,

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:46.160
which means that the
metal from the ax head

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:48.550
goes all the way through the handle,

00:16:48.550 --> 00:16:53.300
then it could be really
well balanced, light, sharp.

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:54.513
Nine out of 10.

00:17:00.054 --> 00:17:01.760
Thor's weapon, Stormbreaker,

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:04.610
could be based on a Viking battle-ax,

00:17:04.610 --> 00:17:07.320
but it looks more like a poleax.

00:17:07.320 --> 00:17:11.850
You have bladed end of the head

00:17:11.850 --> 00:17:16.180
and a hammer-type impact
tool on the opposite end

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:19.400
on the top of a long pole at its core.

00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:20.850
It's believable.

00:17:20.850 --> 00:17:23.020
It's just gone into fantasy territory,

00:17:23.020 --> 00:17:24.550
but it's used the same way.

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:28.470
There's many, many strange
weapons from history.

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:32.890
Not every ax, haft, or
handle was straight.

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:34.165
There's lots of curves,

00:17:34.165 --> 00:17:36.480
and some of them really long curves.

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:39.338
Sometimes they used

00:17:39.338 --> 00:17:41.407
the roots of a wood,

00:17:41.407 --> 00:17:45.550
the natural curve, so that
that curve was strong.

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:48.880
So, the opponent's weapon
is being used against him.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:51.540
That's a really good thing to understand,

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:53.399
that it's not something

00:17:53.399 --> 00:17:55.770
you just want to get out of the way of,

00:17:55.770 --> 00:17:57.730
but you can turn it on your opponent,

00:17:57.730 --> 00:17:59.630
and they show this really well here.

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:09.117
Maybe closer to the ax
head in this instance.

00:18:09.117 --> 00:18:11.750
Use force, maneuverability,

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:14.600
balance, technique, whatever you can

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:18.250
to try and change the
disadvantage into an advantage.

00:18:18.250 --> 00:18:21.350
There's not a lot of
impressive technique here,

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:23.440
but the core basics are there,

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:25.540
so five out of 10.

00:18:25.540 --> 00:18:27.440
Even in a street fight,

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:30.390
people can get caught up in thinking

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:32.460
that this is a contest of strength,

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:35.690
and they both push against each other.

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:38.148
People can freeze. There's
lots of things that can happen.

